from review import card,save,DATE
i='TCS-4031';_,c=card(i);refs=c['references']
refs[0]['locator']='Section 3, page 55:12; Appendix A.2, especially the oracle-circuit and effective-hypothesis definitions on page 55:17.'
refs += [dict(id='full',title='Pseudo-derandomizing learning and approximation — full report',authors='Igor Carboni Oliveira; Rahul Santhanam',year=2018,url='https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2018/122/',pdf_url='https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2018/122/download/',locator='Report posted 3 July 2018; self-learning discussion and learning definitions. No later revision listed on the report page.'),dict(id='thesis',title='The Complexity of Meta-Computational Problems',authors='Ninad Rajgopal',year=2020,url='https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid:f2fe5da6-1b6d-4147-88b5-85e9d818a559',pdf_url='https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid%3Af2fe5da6-1b6d-4147-88b5-85e9d818a559/files/df1881m308',locator='DPhil thesis, title page Trinity 2020; Chapter 3, Lemma 3.11 and concluding discussion on page 36 explicitly retain quasipolynomial AC⁰ self-learning. Repository crawl dates are not the thesis date.')]
notes=['Recovered randomized membership-query learning under uniform input distance; the learner itself and its output hypothesis must both be constant-depth AND/OR/NOT circuits.','Made the source’s fixed-constant accuracy/confidence interpretation explicit; no efficient dependence on inverse accuracy or confidence is asserted.','Specified depth and quasipolynomial-size quantifiers, generation uniformity, oracle depth and an effective layered hypothesis encoding.','Removed the truncated proposed approach and distinguished ordinary time-bounded learning and threshold-augmented self-learning from the requested model.','Checked the full 2018 report and Rajgopal’s 2020 thesis, whose concluding question repeats the AC⁰ target; searched later work.']
save(i,dict(title='Quasipolynomial AC⁰ self-learning',formal='Does AC⁰ admit uniform quasipolynomial-size AC⁰ self-learners? More precisely, for every fixed integers d,k≥1 and fixed rational constants ε,δ∈(0,1/100], do there exist constants D,a≥1 and a uniform family of randomized multi-output oracle AC⁰ circuits Lₙ, of depth at most D and total size at most Q(n)=2^{a⌈log₂(n+2)⌉^a}, such that for every n≥1 and every Boolean function f on n bits with an AC⁰ circuit of depth at most d and at most n^k gates, Lₙ with membership-query oracle f outputs an effective description of a depth-at-most-D AC⁰ hypothesis h of size at most Q(n), with Pr[Pr_{x uniform in {0,1}ⁿ}[h(x)≠f(x)]≤ε]≥1−δ? The outer probability is over the learner’s independent fair random input bits. This is the fixed-constant learning-parameter reading of the source’s self-learning question.',definitions='''An AC⁰ circuit is a finite Boolean directed acyclic circuit with unbounded-fan-in AND and OR gates, unary NOT gates, constants 0 and 1, and input bits. Depth is the greatest number of gates on a directed input-to-output path. Circuit descriptions may be nonuniform for the target function: the guarantee covers every eligible f at each length, not merely one uniformly generated target family. Neither majority/threshold gates nor parity gates are added to the basis.

For the learner, an oracle gate has n ordered Boolean inputs y₁,…,yₙ and returns the bit f(y₁,…,yₙ). Each oracle gate counts as one gate in the depth; its query wires count in the size. Query bits may depend on answers at earlier gates, but the total circuit depth remains bounded by D. The learner is a circuit with multiple output bits and at most Q(n) independent fair random input bits; there is no further source of information about f. Size here bounds gates, wires, input terminals and output terminals together. The number of random bits and all circuit topology are fixed before f is supplied. No unrestricted computation is allowed between queries. The accuracy distribution is uniform on the target’s entire n-bit domain, independently of the learner’s coins. This is not distribution-free PAC learning and does not require a fixed output or fixed queries on most random tapes.

An effective output is an explicit layered description of a Boolean AC⁰ circuit on n bits. One concrete convention uses D ordered layers of padded gate slots, a gate-type field for each slot, Boolean adjacency masks to earlier layers or input terminals, and an output-selector mask. NOT slots have exactly one predecessor; all edges go to later layers. Unused slots are constants. The format has polynomial length in the number of slots, and evaluating a valid encoded circuit on an input is possible by an AC⁰ circuit of depth O(D) and polynomial size in the encoding length. Both the description length and hypothesis gates/wires are bounded by Q(n); invalid descriptions count as failures. Enlarging a and D absorbs the polynomial overhead of this explicit encoding and standard layering. The output h has no f-oracle gates. A constant-depth circuit with only one additional top threshold gate would not meet the stated hypothesis class.

Uniformity means that, for the fixed d,k,ε,δ, one deterministic Turing algorithm given 1ⁿ generates the entire description of Lₙ in time polynomial in Q(n). The algorithm, its polynomial exponent, D and a may depend on those four fixed parameters, but not on n or f. There is no advice depending on f and no claim of polynomial-time generation in n. The source explicitly leaves general ε,δ dependence informal and often fixes sufficiently small constants; the statement adopts that constant-parameter interpretation. It does not demand a single learner whose complexity is polynomial in 1/ε and log(1/δ), nor learner or hypothesis depth equal to target depth. All bounds apply to every n; constant padding can handle small lengths.''',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='A positive answer supplies the uniform oracle-circuit families and proves all depth, size, encoding and probability guarantees for every fixed d,k,ε,δ in the specified ranges and every eligible target. A negative answer disproves this universal existence claim, for example for some fixed parameter choice against all permitted learner depths and quasipolynomial size exponents. Quasipolynomial sequential running time, unrestricted hypothesis circuits, or a threshold-augmented hypothesis class alone do not meet the target.',context_blocks=[dict(title='Learning has its own circuit complexity',text='Self-learning compares the resources needed to infer a function with those needed to evaluate it. The source requires both the learning computation and the generated hypothesis to stay in the same gate class, while allowing larger size and a larger constant depth.',citation='primary'),dict(title='Known time bounds leave this question',text='The source distinguishes this question from known deterministic quasipolynomial-time learning of AC⁰. A sequential time bound does not supply an AC⁰ learner or an AC⁰ representation of its answer.',citation='primary'),dict(title='A nearby threshold-augmented class',text='The 2018 paper observes subexponential self-learning for a class with one majority gate over AC⁰ circuits. Rajgopal’s thesis develops the related threshold-over-AC⁰ result and its consequences for approximate circuit minimization. These are different gate classes and size bounds.',citation='thesis'),dict(title='A later explicit open question',text='The conclusion of Chapter 3 of Rajgopal’s 2020 thesis again asks for quasipolynomial AC⁰ learners for AC⁰. It therefore does not present the threshold-augmented result as a solution of this question.',citation='thesis')],why='Tests whether learning a shallow Boolean computation intrinsically needs stronger gates or greater depth than evaluating it. The restriction links learning complexity with the circuit complexity of approximate minimization and with derandomization, beyond the usual sequential running-time measure.',progress=[dict(date='2018',text='The source asks whether AC⁰ can self-learn with quasipolynomial-size AC⁰ oracle circuits and explicitly separates this from known time-bounded learning.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2020',text='Rajgopal’s thesis connects self-learners to approximate circuit minimization and repeats the quasipolynomial AC⁰ question in its Chapter 3 conclusion.',citation='thesis')],references=refs,status='open',status_note='Read the conference definition and question, the full ECCC report dated 3 July 2018 and the later thesis discussion. Searches through 12 September 2026 found no resolution in this exact learner-and-hypothesis model; they are not exhaustive. The thesis is dated Trinity 2020 on its title page, despite later repository indexing dates.',model_self_contained=True,requires_context=False,review_note='The source calls its general notion informal with respect to ε and δ. This card explicitly adopts its sufficiently-small-constant convention rather than inventing a fully parameter-efficient guarantee. Explicit layered encoding and wire counting instantiate the source’s effective-description requirement with polynomial overhead absorbed in the quasipolynomial bound.',importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='A structural question about whether shallow circuits can infer their own class with similarly shallow computation, connected to circuit minimization and derandomization. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),working_summary=dict(sentences=['The target is to learn any polynomial-size constant-depth Boolean circuit using a constant-depth learner of quasipolynomial size.','The learner can query the unknown function and uses random bits, with approximation measured on uniform inputs.','Its output must itself describe a quasipolynomial-size constant-depth AND/OR/NOT circuit.','Known sequential-time learning and threshold-augmented self-learning do not give this combination.','The card states the source’s fixed-constant accuracy and confidence interpretation and records the same question in a 2020 thesis.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','full','thesis'])),notes=notes,checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs])
